1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,200
So what's going on? Why isn't my face being detected?

2
00:00:03,200 --> 00:00:06,600
Well, we have to look at how we give machine sight.

3
00:00:06,600 --> 00:00:12,000
Computer vision uses machine learning techniques to do facial recognition.

4
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:15,800
So how this works is you create a training set with examples of faces.

5
00:00:15,800 --> 00:00:18,600
This is a face, this is a face, this is not a face.

6
00:00:18,600 --> 00:00:23,200
And over time, you can teach a computer how to recognize other faces.

7
00:00:23,200 --> 00:00:27,200
However, if the training sets aren't really that diverse,

8
00:00:27,400 --> 00:00:32,400
any face that deviates too much from the established norm will be harder to detect,

9
00:00:32,400 --> 00:00:34,400
which is what was happening to me.

10
00:00:34,400 --> 00:00:36,800
But don't worry, there's some good news.

11
00:00:36,800 --> 00:00:39,600
Training sets don't just materialize out of nowhere.

12
00:00:39,600 --> 00:00:41,400
We actually can create them.

13
00:00:41,400 --> 00:00:45,700
So there's an opportunity to create full spectrum training sets

14
00:00:45,700 --> 00:00:49,500
that reflect a richer portrait of humanity.

15
00:00:49,500 --> 00:00:51,700
Now, you've seen it in my examples,

16
00:00:51,700 --> 00:00:58,100
how social robots was how I found out about exclusion with algorithmic bias.

17
00:00:58,100 --> 00:01:02,600
But algorithmic bias can also lead to discriminatory practices.

18
00:01:03,900 --> 00:01:07,600
Across the US, police departments are starting to use

19
00:01:07,600 --> 00:01:11,700
facial recognition software in their crime-fighting arsenal.

20
00:01:12,100 --> 00:01:17,600
Georgetown Law published a report showing that one in two adults in the US,

21
00:01:17,600 --> 00:01:24,500
that's 117 million people, have their faces in facial recognition networks.

22
00:01:24,500 --> 00:01:29,100
Police departments can currently look at these networks unregulated

23
00:01:29,100 --> 00:01:33,400
using algorithms that have not been audited for accuracy.

24
00:01:33,400 --> 00:01:37,300
Yet we know facial recognition is not felt proof

25
00:01:37,300 --> 00:01:41,500
and labeling faces consistently remains a challenge.

26
00:01:41,500 --> 00:01:43,100
You might have seen this on Facebook.

27
00:01:43,100 --> 00:01:46,300
My friends and I laugh all the time when we see other people

28
00:01:46,300 --> 00:01:48,800
mislabeled in our photos.

29
00:01:48,800 --> 00:01:54,400
But misidentifying a suspected criminal is no laughing matter,

30
00:01:54,400 --> 00:01:57,300
nor is breaching civil liberties.

31
00:01:57,300 --> 00:02:00,500
Machine learning is being used for facial recognition,

32
00:02:00,500 --> 00:02:04,900
but it's also extending beyond the realm of computer vision.

33
00:02:05,800 --> 00:02:09,800
In her book, Weapons of Math Destruction,

34
00:02:09,800 --> 00:02:16,500
Data Scientist, Kathy O'Neill talks about the rising new WMDs.

35
00:02:16,500 --> 00:02:20,900
Widespread, mysterious and destructive algorithms

36
00:02:20,900 --> 00:02:23,900
that are increasingly being used to make decisions

37
00:02:23,900 --> 00:02:27,100
that impact more aspects of our lives.

38
00:02:27,100 --> 00:02:28,900
So who gets hired or fired?

39
00:02:28,900 --> 00:02:29,800
Do you get that loan?

40
00:02:29,800 --> 00:02:30,800
Do you get insurance?

41
00:02:30,800 --> 00:02:34,600
Are you admitted into the college that you wanted to get into?

42
00:02:34,600 --> 00:02:38,200
Do you and I pay the same price for the same product

43
00:02:38,200 --> 00:02:40,700
purchased on the same platform?

44
00:02:40,700 --> 00:02:44,400
Law enforcement is also starting to use machine learning

45
00:02:44,400 --> 00:02:46,700
for predictive policing.

46
00:02:46,700 --> 00:02:49,700
Some judges use machine-generated risk scores

47
00:02:49,700 --> 00:02:54,700
to determine how long an individual is going to spend in prison.

48
00:02:54,700 --> 00:02:57,200
So we really have to think about these decisions.

49
00:02:57,200 --> 00:02:58,400
Are they fair?

50
00:02:58,400 --> 00:03:01,300
And we've seen that algorithmic bias

51
00:03:01,300 --> 00:03:04,500
doesn't necessarily always lead to fair outcomes.

